X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =30,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}x\\cdot 4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4{x}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =0.8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.03.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(0.8,\\frac{0.03}{30}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.8,0.001).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.7-0.8}{\\sqrt{0.001}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-3.16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the complement of the normal distribution, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z>-3.16) & =1-P(Z<-3.16) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-3.16).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have that\n\n$$\nΦ(-3.16)=0.0008.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.7) & =1-Φ(-3.16) \\\\  & =1-0.0008 \\\\  & =0.9992.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499561 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499561 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499561 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499561 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.4$ | $0.25$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{128}$ be a random sample of size $n=128$ from a population with the probability mass function given above.  Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is between $3.6$ and $4.2.$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.7\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7580\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7611\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7642\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.8\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7881\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7910\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.7939\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.9\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8159\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8186\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.8212\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th><p></p>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.7\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0446\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0436\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0427\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.6\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0548\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0537\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0526\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.5\\,$</th>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0668\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0655\\,$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0643\\,$ </td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499561 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f56d1332-6e08-590b-aa4f-b85bae891009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499561 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=128,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.4+4\\cdot 0.25+6\\cdot 0.1+8\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0.4+1+0.6+2 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({1}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+{6}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{8}^{2}\\cdot 0.25)-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.4+4+3.6+16)-16 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(4,\\frac{8}{128}) \\\\  & ∼N(4,0.0625).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(3.6<\\overset{―}{X}<4.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3.6<\\overset{―}{X}<4.2) & ≈-P(\\frac{3.6-4}{\\sqrt{0.0625}}<Z<\\frac{4.2-4}{\\sqrt{0.0625}}) \\\\  & ≈-P(-1.6<Z<0.8) \\\\  & =P(Z<0.8)-P(Z<-1.6) \\\\  & =Φ(0.8)-Φ(-1.6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.8)=0.7881, \\\\ Φ(-1.6)=0.0548.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3.6<\\overset{―}{X}<4.2) & =Φ(0.8)-Φ(-1.6) \\\\  & =0.7881-0.0548 \\\\  & =0.7333.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499566 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186499372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499566 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143501" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499566 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499566 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{50}$ be a random sample of size $n=50$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{{x}^{6}}, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.1,$ calculate $P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2).$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1131\\,$ | $\\,0.1112\\,$ | $\\,0.1093\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1335\\,$ | $\\,0.1314\\,$ | $\\,0.1292\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ | $\\,0.1515\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499566 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fc097a1b-4231-5bd2-b0eb-22f13d9b5569" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186499566 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =50,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }x\\cdot \\frac{5}{{x}^{6}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{b}\\frac{5}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{b\\to \\infty }{[-\\frac{5}{4{x}^{4}}]}_{1}^{b} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =1.25,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.1.$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(1.25,\\frac{0.1}{50}) \\\\  & ∼N(1.25,0.002).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2) & =P(Z<\\frac{1.2-1.25}{\\sqrt{0.002}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.12) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have that\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.12)=0.1314.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}<1.2) & =Φ(-1.12) \\\\  & =0.1314.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506674 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186507542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506674 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186507563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506674 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186507575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506674 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186507579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506674 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186507596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506674 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186506674 #edb/ref 1045 "The Central Limit Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17